 ## Overview
-The Multi-Channel Page-Table Hierarchy is a GPU-driven data structure that virtualizes a bricked multi-resolution, multi-channel volume via a paged 3D address space [S1, unofficial][S2]. It solves the problem of managing brick residency and performing address translation for out-of-core volume rendering without traversing a tree structure [S1, unofficial]. Unlike its sibling [Residency Octree](5269-residency-octree.md), which uses an octree for hierarchical culling, the page-table hierarchy treats each brick as an independent chunk, allowing direct access to any brick in the dataset [S1, unofficial]. It extends the single-channel page-table approach by maintaining one page-table hierarchy per channel, all sharing a single brick cache [S1, unofficial][S2].
+The Multi-Channel Page-Table Hierarchy is a GPU-driven data structure that virtualizes a bricked multi-resolution, multi-channel volume via a paged 3D address space [S1, unofficial][S2]. It solves the problem of managing brick residency and performing address translation for out-of-core volume rendering without traversing a tree structure [S1, unofficial]. Unlike the [Residency Octree](5269-residency-octree.md), which uses an octree for hierarchical culling, the page-table hierarchy treats each brick as an independent chunk, allowing direct access to any brick in the dataset [S1, unofficial]. It extends the single-channel page-table approach by maintaining one page-table hierarchy per channel, all sharing a single brick cache [S1, unofficial][S2].
 
